{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01262 - MONDO_15165",
  "verbalization": "Decitabine is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and Myelodysplastic Syndromes (MDS) through its role in DNA methylation inhibition, a mechanism that is well-documented in the context of chemotherapy for these conditions. The evidence suggests that Decitabine may be linked to AML and MDS through its interaction with Ivosidenib, a topoisomerase II inhibitor, which is also associated with these diseases. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Decitabine to AML/MDS, including its indication for Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia and its association with Ivosidenib, supports a potential mechanistic link. The shared indication for Ivosidenib with AML/MDS and the indirect pathway through Decitabine suggest that the therapeutic relationship may involve a broader network of molecular interactions, possibly involving DNA methylation and topoisomerase II inhibition. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Decitabine to influence disease progression through mechanisms that are relevant to AML and MDS.",
